{"command": "sed -n '1,120p' /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "# Feature Specification: Tagged Cache (TaggedCache)\n\n**Feature Branch**: `001-transactions`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-17\n\n**Status**: Draft (updated 2026-10-17 for milestone 2 + round-2 clarification; updated 2026-10-17 for milestone 3 R07/R08 entry expiry)\n\n**Input**: User description: \"Build cachetools.tagged.TaggedCache(maxsize), using the\nexisting cachetools.LRUCache for bounded storage and preserving all existing\ncachetools APIs. R01 put/get/copying/tags validation; R02 invalidate/recency/\neviction hygiene; R03 maxsize validation and atomic replacement. Claim-A\ncandidate extension (variant put_no_recency_refresh, 2026-09-21): R02\nextension - overwriting an existing key via put refreshes its LRU recency, making\nit the most-recently-used entry. Milestone 2 (retaining R01-R03): R04\ninvalidate_many(tags, mode='any'); R05 resize(maxsize) with maxsize property;\nR06 generator tags consumed once + repeated-get/put detachment. Conflicting\nevidence: an old generated design note ('empty all-tags matches every key and\nresizing may flush the cache') is superseded by R04/R05. Claim-A candidate\nextensions, round 2 (2026-09-22): R05 clarification - resize must preserve the\nabsolute expiry of surviving entries (R07/R08 define the expiry as entry\nstate). Milestone 3 (retaining R01-R06): R07 constructor accepts timer=None (default time.monotonic); put adds ttl=None, where None means no expiration and a finite nonnegative int/float excluding bool sets expiry at timer()+ttl; invalid TTL raises ValueError before any mutation; TTL zero is immediately expired; at the exact expiry boundary a key is expired. R08 expired entries are removed before get, put, len, resize, and invalidation, without touching the recency of surviving entries; overwriting an entry replaces its prior expiry; tag invalidation counts only live entries; a missing/expired get raises KeyError; existing callers without timer/ttl retain previous behavior.\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Store and Retrieve Tagged Entries (Priority: P1)\n\nA developer creates a bounded `TaggedCache(maxsize)` and stores values under\nhashable keys with string tags. Retrieval by key returns a detached deep copy\nof the stored value; a missing key raises `KeyError`. Values are copied on\ninput as well, so later mutation of the caller's object does not change the\ncached content. Tags are deduplicated; a bare string or a non-string tag entry\nis rejected with `ValueError` before any existing state changes.\n\n**Why this priority**: Core value of the feature - safe, tagged, bounded\nstorage. Without it nothing else works.\n\n**Independent Test**: Create a cache, put a value with tags, get it back and\nassert equality plus detachment (mutate the returned object, re-get, value\nunchanged); assert `KeyError` for a missing key; assert `ValueError` for\n`tags=\"x\"` and `tags=[1]` and that state is unchanged after rejection.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** an empty `TaggedCache(2)`, **When** `put('a', 1, ['x'])` then\n   `get('a')` returns `1`.\n2. **Given** a cached mutable value, **When** the caller mutates the original\n   object after `put`, **Then** `get` returns the value as stored at insertion\n   (copied on input).\n3. **Given** a cached value, **When** the caller mutates the object returned by\n   `get`, **Then** a subsequent `get` returns the unmutated stored value\n   (detached deep copy).\n4. **Given** any key not present, **When** `get(key)` is called, **Then**\n   `KeyError` is raised.\n5. **Given** a cache with existing entries, **When** `put(key, value, tags)` is\n   called with a bare string as `tags` or a non-string tag entry, **Then**\n   `ValueError` is raised and no existing entries, tags, or recency change.\n6. **Given** `put('a', 1, ['x', 'x', 'y'])`, **When** the entry is inspected\n   via `invalidate`, **Then** the entry is tagged exactly once per distinct\n   tag (`invalidate('x') == 1`, `invalidate('y') == 1`).\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Invalidate by Tag and Correct LRU Eviction (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer removes all currently cached entries carrying a given tag in one\ncall and learns how many were removed. Replacing a key removes its old tag\nmemberships. When the cache is full, the least-recently-used entry is evicted\nand eviction leaves no stale tag memberships that could affect later reuse of\nthe evicted key. `get` refreshes LRU recency. `len(cache)` counts live\nentries.\n\n**Why this priority**: Tag-based bulk removal and eviction correctness are the\nsecond core value; they depend on Story 1's storage but are independently\ntestable.\n\n**Independent Test**: Fill a small cache, call `invalidate(tag)` and assert the\nreturned count and that only tagged entries were removed; put past capacity and\nassert the LRU victim was evicted and that re-putting the same key with new\ntags does not resurrect old memberships.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** entries tagged `'x'` and `'y'`, **When** `invalidate('x')`,\n   **Then** it returns the number of removed entries and only those entries are\n   gone; `invalidate` of an unknown tag returns `0`.\n2. **Given** a key stored with tags `['old']`, **When** the same key is\n   re-put with tags `['new']`, **Then** `invalidate('old')` returns `0` and\n   `get(key)` returns the new value (old memberships removed).\n3. **Given** a full cache, **When** a new entry is put, **Then** the\n   least-recently-used entry is evicted, `len` stays at `maxsize`, and a later\n   `put` of the evicted key with different tags does not carry the old tags.\n4. **Given** a full cache where one entry was just `get`-accessed, **When** a\n   new entry is put, **Then** the accessed entry survives and a different LRU\n   entry is evicted (get refreshes recency).\n5. **Given** any sequence of puts/gets/invalidates, **When** `len(cache)` is\n   called, **Then** it equals the number of live entries and never exceeds\n   `maxsize`.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Configuration and Replacement Safety (Priority: P3)\n\nA developer constructs the cache with a positive integer `maxsize` (booleans\nare rejected) and relies on replacement being atomic: invalid replacement\ninput (bad tags, bad value handling) leaves the previously stored value, tags,\nand recency exactly as they were. Every value costs exactly one capacity slot\nregardless of object size.\n\n**Why this priority**: Guards against data corruption and misconfiguration;\nimportant but exercised less frequently than put/get/invalidate.\n\n**Independent Test**: Assert `TaggedCache(0)`, `TaggedCache(-1)`,\n`TaggedCache(True)`, `TaggedCache(2.0)` raise `ValueError`; put a valid entry,\nattempt `put` with invalid tags, and assert the old value/tags/recency are\nunchanged; put `maxsize` large objects and confirm the (maxsize+1)th evicts the\nLRU entry (one slot per value).\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n"}